"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 4.3 and the pathological findings showing Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with pT2 disease. Positive prognostic indicators include negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no documented lymph node metastasis. The lack of biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison data limits assessment regarding potential discrepancies in grading or staging. Missing postoperative PSA information prevents evaluation of its current trajectory, which is critical for risk stratification. Future prognosis revision would be significantly aided by subsequent postoperative PSA levels and their pattern over time. Given the documented negative margins and node status, these factors are favorable. The uncertainty surrounding future recurrence prediction remains due to the absence of longitudinal biochemical data. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 67.81–101.71 months; its coverage has not been validated."
